# General Branch Coverage Guide for Java Tests

This rule documents the required branch coverage and test patterns for achieving 100% coverage in any Java test suite, focusing on:

- All branches for null, empty, and edge-case inputs
- All logical paths in comparators and conditionals (e.g., true/false branches, &&/|| logic)
- Validation failure and exception branches
- Both directions of comparator logic (e.g., a > b and b > a)

## Test Patterns
- Use mocks to simulate all type and value combinations for method arguments
- Use reflection to invoke private methods for direct branch testing if needed
- Ensure both directions of comparator and conditional logic are exercised
- Cover all edge cases for null, empty, and unexpected input values
- Test exception and error branches explicitly

## Example Test Scenarios
- Both arguments satisfy a compound condition (e.g., both true for &&)
- Only one argument satisfies a compound condition (e.g., true/false and false/true for &&)
- Neither argument satisfies a compound condition (both false)
- Different values for comparator branches (e.g., a > b, a < b, a == b)
- Inputs that trigger exception or error handling
- Inputs that are null or empty collections
- Inputs that are not of the expected type

See your project's test files for implementation examples and adapt these patterns to your codebase for comprehensive coverage.


